-- title:
--   Transport of the Drinfeld Tate representation along a constant-field isomorphism
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime and let $k$, $k'$ be fields equipped with $\mathbb{F}_{q^2} =$ `GaloisField q 2`-algebra structures such that the Drinfeld coordinate rings `CoordRing q k` and `CoordRing q k'`, that is the quotients of $k[X_0,X_1]$, resp. $k'[X_0,X_1]$, by the principal ideal generated by `drinfeldPoly q · - 1`, are domains; let $e : k \simeq k'$ be an $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$-algebra isomorphism. Let $\ell$ be a prime, $E$ a field equipped with a $\mathbb{Q}_\ell$-algebra structure, and $S$ an arbitrary type. Write $V_\ell(k) = \mathbb{Q}_\ell \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_\ell} T_\ell\bigl(\mathrm{Pic}^0(k, F_k)\bigr)$, where $F_k$ is the fraction field of `CoordRing q k` and $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ is the group of degree-zero divisors modulo principal divisors, and similarly for $k'$. The assertion is that there is an $E$-linear isomorphism $\Theta$ from $S \to E \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}_\ell} V_\ell(k)$ onto $S \to E \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}_\ell} V_\ell(k')$ such that for every element $h$ of `hSubgroup q` — the kernel of the character of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/q) \times \mathbb{F}_{q^2}^\times$ sending $(g,\alpha)$ to the image of $\det g$ in $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}^\times$ times $\alpha^{q+1}$ — one has the equality of $E$-linear maps $\Theta \circ \rho_k(h) = \rho_{k'}(h) \circ \Theta$, where $\rho_k(h) =$ `tateProdRep q k ℓ E S h` acts coordinatewise on $S$ by `tateRep q k ℓ E h`, the base change to $E$ of the rational Galois representation of the automorphism group of $F_k$ over $k$ on $V_\ell(k)$ evaluated at `hFunctionFieldAction q k h`, and likewise for $k'$.
--
--   This is the functoriality of the Drinfeld-curve Tate representation in the constant field: an isomorphism of the base fields over $\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$ induces an isomorphism of the $\ell$-adic representations of the pairs $(g,\alpha)$ with $\det g \cdot \alpha^{q+1} = 1$ on the (product over $S$ of the) rational Tate modules of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the curve $xy^q - x^q y = 1$. It is used by the full-level Tate module statements [`FullLevelTate.exists_linearMap_tateProd_comp_baseChange_eq_and_eq_zero_of_five_le`](thm.html#FullLevelTate.exists_linearMap_tateProd_comp_baseChange_eq_and_eq_zero_of_five_le) and its companions for the cases $\ell = 2, 3$, in order to move the Drinfeld specialisation from one algebraically closed constant field to another.
